			"author": {
				"name": "cthos 🐱",
				"url": "https://mastodon.cthos.dev/@cthos",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/322/028/040/622/495/original/af321586b1f6cd2d.webp"
			},
			"date": "2024-08-04T01:25:27.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> I started to walk through (in addition to what you said above) and I have a couple of wild speculations. They could also be doing a \"keep warm\" tactic where they invoke the function to keep it awake periodically to eliminate cold starts.</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "112901185752080167",
			"parentId": "112901143163046690",
			"url": "https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat/112901185752080167",
			"author": {
				"name": "Zach Leatherman",
				"url": "https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/accounts/avatars/109/286/461/031/266/152/original/dc489c3746a44e00.png"
			},
			"date": "2024-08-04T01:36:18.297Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://mastodon.cthos.dev/@cthos\" class=\"u-url mention\">@<span>cthos</span></a></span> oh huh! How warm should the start be 😅</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "112901195849434035",
			"parentId": "112901185752080167",
			"url": "https://mastodon.cthos.dev/@cthos/112901195803612836",
			"author": {
				"name": "cthos 🐱",
				"url": "https://mastodon.cthos.dev/@cthos",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/322/028/040/622/495/original/af321586b1f6cd2d.webp"
			},
			"date": "2024-08-04T01:38:51.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> Depends on how \"critical path\" the function is. AWS will keep a lambda function \"warm\" for a period of time until it stops getting requests and then it'll put it to sleep. It'll incur a performance penalty to wake it back up. How long that penalty is depends a lot on the function and the language the function is written in. It can be as long as a full second though. One common tactic is to just have a cron job go ping the service to keep it alive every couple of minutes.</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "112901199143633860",
			"parentId": "112901195849434035",
			"url": "https://mastodon.cthos.dev/@cthos/112901199100180455",
			"author": {
				"name": "cthos 🐱",
				"url": "https://mastodon.cthos.dev/@cthos",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/322/028/040/622/495/original/af321586b1f6cd2d.webp"
			},
			"date": "2024-08-04T01:39:41.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> Related fun, there are like... at least 2 other AWS addons for solving this problem in various ways (managed provisioning and SnapStart for Java).</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
